  "response": "The debate over whether the Liang Bua hominins represented a new species or a pathological population of Homo sapiens rests on the history of the site's fieldwork. The Liang Bua remains were first discovered in 1998, before a joint Australian-Indonesian team excavated them in 2003. That excavation cut through roughly six metres of fine-grained cave sediment on Flores. It recovered stone artefacts, the bones of dwarfed Stegodon, and fragmentary hominin elements from several individuals. Most of the interpretive weight, however, fell on a single individual. The most complete skeleton recovered at Liang Bua is catalogued as LB1.\n\nLB1 stood about 1.06 metres tall; in other words, its standing height was approximately 106 centimetres. That figure lies below the adult average of any healthy living population of Homo sapiens. Its cranial capacity, estimated at around 400 cubic centimetres, was similarly modest and comparable to that of a chimpanzee. Dimensions of this kind admitted two incompatible readings, and the discipline spent more than a decade weighing them.\n\nIndonesian paleoanthropologist Teuku Jacob argued that LB1 was a modern human with microcephaly, a developmental disorder that reduces the size of the brain and skull. Testing that diagnosis required a reliable picture of the brain itself, and here the specimen's completeness was beside the point. Dean Falk's virtual endocast study concluded that LB1's brain did not match the brain of a microcephalic modern human. Its frontal and temporal morphology departed from the pathological pattern rather than reproducing it. Postcranial anatomy pointed the same way. Analysis of the Liang Bua wrist bones found a primitive trapezoid shape unlike that of modern humans, a configuration shared with African apes and early hominins and not produced by known growth disorders. The endocast result confirms the conclusion that Peter Brown and colleagues had reached when they named the species Homo floresiensis in a 2004 paper in Nature.\n\nThe primitive carpal morphology indicates that LB1's mandible had a chin. The jaw, however, admits exactly one of two descriptions: either it had a chin, or LB1's mandible lacked a chin, and these two accounts cannot both be true. Because of that chinless jaw, the small body size of Homo floresiensis resulted partly from insular dwarfing on Flores. Insular dwarfing is the process by which island populations shrink under limited resources and reduced predation. It need not be the only process at work. At least one of two explanations holds, perhaps both: insular dwarfing, or descent from a small-bodied ancestor. On the second explanation, the small body size of Homo floresiensis was partly inherited from a small-bodied ancestral hominin lineage, one that may have left Africa before the emergence of Homo erectus. Although such an inherited stature implies a deep and ancient ancestry, the Liang Bua skeletal remains were dated to about 5,000 years ago. A date that recent placed the hominins alongside modern human farmers in the region.\n\nThe tension between deep ancestry and an apparently recent age was settled by re-examining the cave's stratigraphy. Excavators found that the sediments beneath the skeletons had been eroded and then overlain by much younger deposits, so that the earlier samples had been drawn from the wrong layers. The 2016 revised dating study in Nature concluded that the Liang Bua skeletal remains were about 100,000 to 60,000 years old. This range combines uranium-series dating of the bones with luminescence and argon dating of the surrounding sediments. It removes any need to reconcile an archaic anatomy with an implausibly young age. Combined with the endocast and wrist evidence, the revised chronology leaves the microcephaly hypothesis without anatomical or temporal support. The Liang Bua hominins therefore stand as a distinct species rather than a diseased population of modern humans.",
